## Publishing with `dotnet publish -r <rid>`

The native binary in this package is built against Ubuntu-specific library
versions (ffmpeg, GTK, ICU, and others), so it is intentionally *not*
published under a portable RID such as `linux-x64` -- doing so would let it
install on other distros where it would fail at runtime instead of at
install/build time.

For a normal build or `dotnet run` (no explicit `-r`), no extra configuration
is needed: this package's own `build\*.props` sets `<UseRidGraph>true</UseRidGraph>`
automatically, and the native library is copied to your output folder as part
of the build.

If you publish with an explicit runtime identifier, e.g.

```
dotnet publish -r ubuntu-x64
```

you additionally need to add the following to your own `.csproj` (or a
`Directory.Build.props`):

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <RuntimeIdentifier>ubuntu-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
  <UseRidGraph>true</UseRidGraph>
</PropertyGroup>
```

This is required because NuGet's runtime-asset selection for a publish-time
RID happens during restore, before any package's own build props are
imported -- so a package cannot set this property for you in that scenario.

Note that the `RuntimeIdentifier` value is the plain `ubuntu-x64`, **not**
the versioned package name (e.g. `ubuntu-24.04-x64`). The version in the
package name only tells you which native binary to install (matching your
target Ubuntu release); the `RuntimeIdentifier` your project targets stays
`ubuntu-x64` regardless of which versioned package you reference.

Publishing to a generic RID (`dotnet publish -r linux-x64`) is not supported
by this package: publish-time asset selection can only narrow from a generic
RID to a more specific one, not the other way around, and this package
deliberately does not ship its native binary under a generic RID.

